DirecTV still holds all the cards in Sunday NFL Ticket

JEFFREY FLANAGAN


As the NFL season approaches, the hundreds of thousands of us in Kansas
City without DirecTV again will be without the Sunday NFL Ticket the
package that allows true NFL junkies a chance to watch any NFL game they
want.

And don't expect any relief soon.

DirecTV holds the exclusive overall rights for the NFL Sunday Ticket
through the 2005 season (and exclusive satellite rights through 2007).

Yet don't be surprised if, after the 2005 season, cable operators such as
Time Warner and Comcast  the two largest cable outlets in the Kansas City
area  get heavily involved in the bidding for the Sunday Ticket.

The NFL's skyrocketing popularity will demand it.

I'm sure it will be discussed next time around, said Carol Rothwell, vice
president of public affairs for Time Warner. The last time, it was just too
expensive.

Time Warner, the largest cable provider in the Kansas City area, already
has similar packages for the NBA, NHL and Major League Baseball. It seems
only logical that the NFL would be next.

All of sports programming is getting so expensive, Rothwell said. There
needs to a system for viewers and fans to choose them a la carte.

Time Warner also continues to negotiate a deal with The NFL Network, the
24-hour NFL channel launched last year.

We don't have a master agreement yet, but we're working on it, Rothwell
said.


